    "text": "Now zoom out to a crowd. Billions and billions of pieces, all jiggling. And something new appears, that no single piece has. Watch a drop of ink in water. Each little piece just wanders about, bumping around. None of them is trying to spread out. But the whole drop does. And it never gathers itself back up. Why not? Nothing forbids it. Each piece could wander back. Here's the reason. Count the ways. There is only one way for every piece to be in the left half. But there are a huge number of ways for them to be about half and half. So half and half is what you nearly always get. With just one hundred pieces, the chance of finding them all on one side is one in a million, million, million, million, million. And a glass of water has far more pieces than that. So the rule here isn't really a rule. It's just very, very good odds.",
    "scene": "THE CROWD (why things spread out). 300 specks of ink start in a small drop in a box of water and each takes small random steps, worked out here in the page: nothing tells them to spread, and yet the drop spreads and does not gather itself again. A tally shows how many are in the left half, and how many different ways there are to have that many on the left (the number of ways to choose them). Then the counting is shown with four specks: one way to have all four on the left, six ways to have two and two. With 100 specks there is still one way to be all on the left, against about 10 to the power 29 ways to be half and half, so the chance of finding all 100 on one named side is 1 in 2 to the power 100: 1 in 1,267,650,600,228,229,401,496,703,205,376. A glass of water holds about 8 million billion billion molecules (250 grams; our arithmetic). The spreading is not a law each speck obeys: it is odds."
