    "text": "On the front, things spread out, and never came back. But wait. Water freezes into neat rings. Seeds grow into trees. You tidy your room. Things do get neater. So here is the opposite of spreading out. Order. And how it's allowed. Watch water freezing. As the pieces lock into place, they give off heat. That heat goes into the air around them, and makes the air jiggle more. The inside got tidier. The outside got messier. And if you add it up, the outside always gets messier by more than the inside got neat. That's the deal, as far as anyone has ever seen. You can have order in one spot, if you pay for it somewhere else. A freezer pays with the warm air behind it. You pay with the food you eat. And nearly all of it, in the end, is paid for by the Sun.",
    "scene": "THE REVERSE OF SPREADING OUT: order that pays its way. Three everyday cases of things getting tidier (water freezing, a seed growing, a room being tidied). Then water freezing, drawn with an inside and an outside: as the inside locks into a neat pattern it gives off heat, which makes the air outside jiggle more. The tally uses real numbers for one gram of water freezing at 0 degrees Celsius in a freezer at minus 10: the water's entropy (its amount of mixed-up- ness) falls by 1.22 joules per kelvin, the freezer air's rises by 1.27, so the total goes up by 0.05 (the heat given off, 333.55 joules, divided by each temperature in kelvins; our arithmetic). Order in one place is paid for by more disorder elsewhere, and the total has never been seen to go down. Then who pays: a freezer warms the air behind it, a person runs on food, and nearly all of it on Earth is paid for by sunlight."
