    "sub": "the rule for things our size · left alone, a moving thing keeps moving, forever · a push doesn't keep it going: a push changes it",
    "text": "Now we are up at the size of things you can hold. And the rule here sounds wrong at first. A thing that's moving keeps moving. In a straight line. At the same speed. Forever. It doesn't need a push to keep going. It only needs a push to change. To speed up, to slow down, or to turn. And the heavier it is, the harder it is to change. You've never seen this, because on Earth, something always gets in the way. So let's go where nothing does. This is Voyager One. It left Earth in nineteen seventy seven. Its engines finished their work long ago. And it's still going, at about seventeen kilometers every second. Nothing is pushing it. Nothing needs to. It is now so far away that its radio signal takes a whole day to reach us. And it will keep going long after everyone alive today is gone. That's what moving things do, when you leave them alone.",
    "scene": "MOTION (Newton's first two laws). A puck gliding where nothing touches it leaves marks at equal spacing: same speed, straight line, with no push. Three short cases show what a push does: from behind it speeds up, from in front it slows, from the side it turns. Two balls given the same push show that the heavier one changes less (four times the weight, a quarter of the change). Then a real example of motion left alone: Voyager 1, launched in 1977, its rocket burns long finished, still travelling at about 17 kilometres every second; on 18 November 2026 NASA put it one light-day from Earth (about 25.9 billion kilometres), so its radio signal takes about a day to arrive. A counter adds 17 kilometres a second while you watch."
