    "text": "The front said moving things never stop. So why does everything around you stop? Here is the catch. Friction. Slide the same block across three floors, with the same shove. On ice, it glides a long way. On wood, not so far. On rubber, it stops almost at once. The block didn't run out of push. Something was pushing back, the whole time. Look very closely at where they touch. Nothing is really smooth. The tiny bumps catch on each other, and the atoms on one side tug at the atoms on the other. All that tugging turns the motion into jiggling. Into heat. Rub your hands together, and you can feel it. So friction isn't a different rule. It's one more push, that always points backward. And it isn't the bad guy. Without it, your feet would slide out from under you. You couldn't take a single step.",
    "scene": "THE REVERSE OF \"KEEPS GOING\": friction. The same block is given the same shove (2 metres a second) across three floors, and its slide is worked out here from the real rule (it stops after its speed squared, divided by twice the friction number times gravity, 9.81): on ice (friction number about 0.03) it travels 6.8 metres in 6.8 seconds; on wood (about 0.3) 0.68 metres; on rubber (about 0.7) 0.29 metres. The friction numbers are typical measured values and vary with the surfaces. An arrow shows friction as a push on the block that always points against the motion. A close-up of the two surfaces shows what it is: nothing is perfectly smooth, the high points catch, and the atoms of one surface tug on those of the other (electric forces); the block's motion is not lost but turned into heat, as a pair of bars shows (motion going down, warmth going up, the total unchanged). Last: friction is also what lets a foot push off the ground, and without it there is no walking."
