    "text": "Around every electric charge, there is something invisible that fills the space. It's called a field. You can't see it, but you can see what it does. It's why a magnet can tug a paperclip without touching it. Now, give the charge a shake. The field can't change everywhere at once. So a ripple runs outward through it. That ripple is light. Radio is the same ripple, stretched out long. X rays are the same ripple, squeezed up tight. The colors you can see are just a tiny slice in the middle. And here is the rule at this level. In empty space, that ripple always goes at exactly the same speed. Nearly three hundred thousand kilometers, every second. Sunlight still takes about eight minutes to get here. And nothing that weighs anything at all can ever go that fast. Not because we haven't tried hard enough. From everything we've measured, the universe just doesn't allow it.",
    "scene": "LIGHT. An electric charge with its field drawn as lines running outward. When the charge is shaken, each part of every line follows what the charge did a moment earlier (the further out, the longer ago), so a kink travels out along the lines: that travelling ripple in the field is light. A strip then shows the same ripple at very different lengths: radio (about 3 metres for FM), microwaves (about 12 centimetres in an oven), visible light (380 to 750 billionths of a metre, the only part eyes can see) and X-rays (about a tenth of a billionth of a metre). The speed in empty space is exactly 299,792,458 metres a second, the same for all of them. Sunlight crosses the 150 million kilometres to Earth in about 8 minutes 20 seconds (shown as a moving pulse and a clock). Last, a speed gauge: anything that has weight can be pushed closer and closer to that speed and never reach it."
